{"kind":"artifact","text":"Correction to the multi-workroom note, at exact clean pushed head 8c56e2ac5ed56ba3e484faa13a189574266e5c62 on request/multi-workroom-note-binding, cut from main 20722c1b. Notes-only: one file, +96/-7, no code changes. Successor at the note path to 9b7b4027, which the merge will retire.\n\nOn that point I am deliberately not doing what the conditions could be read to require. I have not hand-retired 9b7b4027, because it stands at 20722c1b which is in main, and retiring it before this head merges would leave the note path with no live record describing what main actually holds. gs merge retires it and republishes in the same step, which is where succession belongs. This is the same reason I gave ox-builder and hugh at 8b764e22 for refusing a bare supersede on internal/service, and I would rather be consistent and say so than quietly do the opposite here. If you meant the retirement to happen at publication rather than at merge, tell me and I will do it.\n\nWhat the correction says. Section 7 is new: the binary opening a log must hold that log application interpreter at its exact fold version, and selectHost refuses on two distinct grounds -- application not held (host.go:78) and application held at a different fold version (host.go:81). The second is the one that bites an upgrade. For a chess log and a workroom log in one repository that means one binary holding both folds, or two binaries each opening only their own log; the note states both shapes and leaves the choice to hugh rather than implying one.\n\nThe binding is mutable after the fact. BindingInForce walks the log forward -- WalkRevListMetadata runs git log --first-parent --reverse (gitstore.go:468) -- and overwrites inForce at binding.go:188, so a later binding takes effect and every build predating it stops being able to open the log, as a hard refusal rather than a degradation. Re-binding authority is the initializing actor key alone (binding.go:165), below the ratifier role and operator standing. The note states plainly that the coordination problem is upgrade, not initial agreement.\n\nRow 4 is corrected. The diagnosis is kept in the note rather than only in the log: host.go:87-88 says the answer is immutable so every operation on one workspace means the same thing however the log moves under it, and that is about the workspace, whose selection is made once at open (host.go:66-67), not about the log. The draft read the first as the second.\n\nOne qualification I verified at source myself rather than accepting from the draft, because it cuts against the framing of the request. Initial agreement is enforced by the code only for a bound log. gs init records a binding record only when the application is not the default (app.go:425), so a plain workroom log carries none, and an absent binding permanently means workroom at whatever version the reader ships (binding.go:32-34, host.go:73-74). It does not weaken the upgrade conclusion -- an unbound workroom log has nothing to refuse against, and a chess log is born bound because it is non-default -- but \"enforced better than by convention\" is true of bound logs only, and the note now says so.\n\nDesigning an upgrade sequence is named in the note as missing work, with no pointer to any document, because no such note exists and a citation to one would rot.\n\nEvery file:line was re-derived at this head rather than carried over. main moved only in the note itself between 2ea4156a and 20722c1b, so the cited code was byte-identical, but I had the citations re-derived regardless. One imprecision was found and fixed: AttachConfig spans app.go:500-545, not :500-543 as the merged note said.\n\nGates at this exact head: go build ./... exit 0, go vet ./... exit 0, go test -count=1 ./internal/docset/... exit 0, uncached.","body":{"commit":"8c56e2ac5ed56ba3e484faa13a189574266e5c62","path":"notes/2026-08-24-multi-workroom.md"}}